<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ubi caritas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/796">796 CE</a> (attributed)</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ubi caritas et amor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Ubi caritas&quot; is a hymn of the Western Church whose text is attributed to Paulinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in 796 CE; its traditional melody probably also comes from the late 8th century. For a long time it was one of the antiphons sung at the washing of feet on Maundy Thursday, part of the Holy Thursday evening liturgy, and it is now and then sung at Eucharistic Adoration and at Benediction of the Blessed Sacrament.</p></div>
